A||||14||MooCow||How can I convince my parents??????????||||8||
Z||000000||MooCow||03-20-2000||05:38 PM||||As of right now I just can't seem to convince my parents to let me get a Fiero.  The insurance is only 400 more a year for full coverage than what I am paying right now.  They said even if I got a better job I still can't get it because they dont make any body pannels for them and they always break down.  Also no one around here no's how to work on them and you have no money so if it breaks then you will have to pay an arm and a leg for it to become repaired.<BR>Is all of that above true?????<BR>I have found 2 Fiero's in my neck of the woods for sale but have yet to get them to even ride in one with me to show them how awsome they really are.  How can I convince them to just even get in one to ride it?? And to let me be able to get one??????<BR>                                 Thanks ken||||reg||8
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||03-20-2000||08:51 PM||||  Ok MOOCOW, heres what you do. You invite your parents to the one place that will convince them that you are on the right track. Let them into the forum with you, so they can see first hand the amount of shared knowledge(more than your average GM mechanic would know) and support that lives here. Let them experience the true virtue of Fierodom.<BR>The reason people have those oppinions(predjudice) is ignorance. Not stupidity, ignorance; they just haven't taken the time to investigate, and, like human nature would dictate, we tend to trust in what we here from "The Experts". The Fiero is a grand example of such injustice. Willingness coupled with the experience of this forum will make even the most diehard skeptic turn.<BR>I myself had about given up on our plastic friend untill I found the forum; now I'm a full fledged Fieroholic. I almost sold my 85GT for $300.00 to a guy at work. With the help of the forum and about $200.00 I now own an awesome driving machine, and, it just keeps getting better. So, ask them to experience the forum for a while with an open mind;  they'll see, there's nothing to worry about with the Greatest little sports car in the world today. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanx, Bam Out! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||Cooter||03-20-2000||10:07 PM||||Sorry to hear your misfortune. Maybe you could convince them how safe the car is. It was rated on the same level of occupant safety as the Volvo 240 Sedan. As far as body panels, they should never need replacing unless an accident causes severe damage. Cost for replacement parts? They share the front suspension with a Chevette. Remember those? They are cheap. The rear suspension shares many components with several front wheel drive GM cars. Very few driveline parts are unique to the Fiero. Breakdowns? Where are you going to find a car that is at least 12 years old that doesnt need repairs? Good luck! But, if your parents are absolutly set against you having a Fiero, just wait a few years until you can afford one on your own.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-20-2000||11:18 PM||||Well, it's true that it's hard to find a mechanic that knows how to work on them and is willing to work on them. I'm reluctant to recommend Fieros to people who aren't "car guys". You know, those who can't or won't do any basic maintenance, let alone minor repair work. Let's be realistic here. Fieros do require some upkeep. Those that own Fieros, but prefer to let the shop work on them, usually can afford to do this. Shop rates range from $25 to $50 an hour. Parts are available, just not always cheap or easy to find.||||reg||1
Z||000004||BAM-BAM||03-20-2000||11:32 PM|||| Batboy, last time I checked (about three weeks ago) shop rates here in "SIN CITY" were<BR>around $65.00 to $80.00 an hour <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. Thusly, I have entered the relm of "FIERO MECHANIC" (with you guys help of course) Bat is right, a Fiero isn't for the timid or short of funds. You must posess one or the other; the willingness to do what you must, or, the cash flow to trust someone else. I prefer to take matters into my own hands. But, I will confess, I learn all I know about Fiero's right here <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">. That, with a basic ability and undestanding, have made the difference <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <P> Be Cool and Good Luck!!!!!!!!!!!!<BR>  Bam Out <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTBro||03-21-2000||12:13 AM||||I just recently got my first Fiero.  I personally don't understand why people have such a negative attitude towards the little car.  I absolutely love it.  I went from my first car... (which was a 4 cyl. Sunbird) to my V6 1986.5 Fiero GT.  I love the raw power I have at the touch of the gas pedal.<P>I can't imagine what a V8 would be like.<P>I bought my Fiero off a mechanic who didn't take care of the poor thing.  It barely ran as I drove it 30 miles home.  It even quit on me once on the drive home.<P>The only thing I have done to it since I bought it last December is: change oil and ALL filters, run Berryman's Complete Fuel System treatment through it, and replaced the alternator.  That's all I have done and it runs like a champ...<P>I think Fiero's are perfectly safe and VERY fun to drive.  Some people have mistaken it for a Porsche (Especially with the Porsche emblem the previous owner put on the front)  The first time I took my baby on the highway, a red Mustang came up beside me, slowed down and started reving his engine.  He wanted to race.  So I kicked his butt.  I love my little car.<P>Sorry for the long post.  (I can be long-winded sometimes)||||reg||12
Z||000006||Uaana||03-21-2000||02:33 AM||||And one more safety example..<BR>Just found out about this one myself..<BR>Go to your local bone yard..<BR>Take a look at all the cars that have been<BR>in side or front collisions and look at the<BR>shape of the interior..(most look pretty messed up). Then find the Fieros.. I found 4<BR>and everyone of them I was able to get into<BR>and sit comfortably. Whoever was in them <BR>probably walked away from their misfortune.<P>Also there are a couple of older threads on this board.. One guy talked about his fiero<BR>being broadsided, slammed into a building, and they drove it onto the trailer...<BR>nuff said...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||03-21-2000||03:08 AM||||Uaana - <BR>Slightly off topic, but where was that boneyard?  I've been looking for some around here but haven't been able to find any... thanks!  <IMG SRC="http://24.8.34.90/faces/lickout.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Uaana||03-21-2000||03:21 AM||||Standard<BR>It's on 169<BR>some dinky little town about 4mi past<BR>Brooklyn Park.. <BR>3 SE's and 1 ?? could have been an 86Gt<BR>About all thats left of that one is the front<BR>aero bumper (not the GT bumper) But for misc<BR>small pieces they are there to be had..<BR>I needed a couple of taillight sockets<BR>guys just said take em... God I love people <BR>that think there just giving away junk. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||03-21-2000||09:05 AM||||Remind your parents that Fieros use common GM FWD powertrains - they're just located in the opposite end of the car.  Most Fiero repairs are as easy or easier to do than similar repairs on other cars.  If your folks wouldn't object to you buying a 1986 Pontiac 6000, they shouldn't object to you buying a 1986 Pontiac Fiero - the reliability will be about the same.  And finding body panels isn't a problem (but don't hit anything and you won't need 'em!)<P>Fieros don't "always break down."  But every Fiero is at least 12 years old, so if you buy one, sooner or later it will need service.  Do you have any interest in learning to work on cars?  Do you have any friends who are knowledgeable shadetree mechanics?  If your answers are yes, don't be afraid to buy a Fiero!  With a good manual, a floor jack, jackstands and a decent set of hand tools, you can fix almost anything.<P>Lastly, I assume the Fiero will be your only car.  Be sure and buy the best-running Fiero you can afford (not the fastest, not the sharpest - the best-running).  And DON'T buy a Fiero with a bad clutch unless you have $1000 to burn!  If it doesn't run and drive like it should, walk away - they made more than 300,000 of these little cars and there's a well-maintained one somewhere out there for you.<P>Good luck, Ken!||||reg||1
Z||000010||MooCow||03-21-2000||10:58 AM||||Hey thanks guys/gals for all the wonderful,helpful posts.  I was starting to drift away from thess little Fiero's, but for some reason they are always in my head.  Everytime I go to school I drive out of my way to look at a Fiero everyday.  I'm only 17 and I think I'm hooked on them already.  Yes I do have an interest in learning how to fix cars.  <BR>     I guess all these arguments that have been going around had gotton to me.  I mean I almost bought a Honda CRX.  Going to talk to the dealer today to see what kind of warrenty is on that Fiero of his.  Thanks again for all.<BR>                                 Thanks Ken||||reg||10
Z||000011||Rodrv6||03-21-2000||10:07 PM||||MooCow, <BR>I have had a Fiero since February, 1984.  I have put 328,000 miles on it since new and I feel that the car is no less reliable than anything else I could have bought at that time.  Mechanically the car is a typical General Motors product and is no more difficult to work on than a Celebrity.  Parts are plentiful and mostly reasonably priced at almost any auto parts store.  Over the years I have changed a few clutches, rebuilt the engine, rebuilt the transaxle, and have done all the usual routine stuff--brakes, water pumps, etc.  Two years ago I decided to do a complete rebuild due to the high number of miles. I completely dissassembled the entire car down to the bare frame.  I even went as far as removing all the wiring harnesses!  Every part that could be unbolted was removed and inspected for condition. (I'm an airplane mechanic and tend to go a little overboard.)  All I found was some rust on the battery tray and some worn suspension parts--ball joints, tie rod ends and steering rack bushings.  Everything else was in good shape.  I spent about $800 including paint for the body panels to put the car back togethor.  I had no difficulty at all doing the teardown and rebuild and have a car that I would be comfortable taking on a long trip with no worries.  If you want a Fiero, I say go for it!!!!||||reg||10
